{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"localstorage-second-oracle-review-2026-10-08","children":[{"type":"text","value":"LocalStorage second oracle review, 2026-10-08"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The four independent reviews examined PR #2074 at\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"9344612bed911a364af6c9c3f9b023487cf6979a"},{"type":"text","value":". The normal merge of\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":" produced "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d5205b792e34f0f6f0282b7811a5c7de712ae0cd"},{"type":"text","value":";\nall changed oracle histories were first run against that merged implementation.\nThe repaired implementation reviewed here is\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1f103c8504d135c1fa35215efba9d7ebc5db92e2"},{"type":"text","value":" (the versioned record itself is a subsequent documentation-only\ncommit).\nThe append-only raw intake is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/private/tmp/pr2074-round2-review-ledger.md"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reviewer-assessment-and-loss-audit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewer assessment and loss audit"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These reviews were accurate and useful. Three reviewers independently found\nthe same parser-normalization gap, which raises confidence but counts as one\nproduct cause. The queue reviewer separated accepted local work from a later\nAPI failure rather than incorrectly demanding cancellation. The key findings\nalso distinguished a serializable NaN-derived key from a nonfinite value that\nJSON cannot round-trip. Proposed fixes were mostly narrow; canonical-key\nrejection was stronger than the existing legacy compatibility contract.\nTechnical depth and signal-to-noise were high. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hire recommendation: yes."}]}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ID"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Technical verdict and evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PR action"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable value"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-RC1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed: unchanged-token normalization of an untouched row left writer and peer stale at the fulfilled receipt."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed in peer oracle and content comparison."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paired changed/unchanged token histories."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-PE1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Same confirmed cause, independently probed."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Duplicate of R2-RC1."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retain independent receipt evidence."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-PA1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed: postwrite custom-parser read fault rejected a durable write."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by validating serialized custom-parser bytes before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setItem"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prewrite and postwrite fault-position histories."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-Q1"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" failure settled before its previously accepted queued write. The later write itself is allowed by the mutations guide."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed in transaction settlement and order oracle."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Held and released receipt cuts with durable, public and restore observations."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-PA2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"n:1"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"n:01"},{"type":"text","value":" silently collapsed into one Map identity."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by rejecting duplicate decoded identities before readiness."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Malformed snapshot grammar retains legacy single-key support."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-PE2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed: a serializable row deriving numeric NaN as its Collection key wrote successfully but could not restore."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed with SameValueZero identity validation."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"NaN, finite number, and string controls."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-PE3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed wording gap: guide implied legacy unprefixed keys were invalid."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed guide wording."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explicit compatibility boundary."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-RC2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed review evidence gap for the previous changed head."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed with this versioned exact-implementation review."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 outcomes below."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-PA3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Same confirmed normalization cause, independently probed."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Duplicate of R2-RC1."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retain the parser-focused probe."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-Q2"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed: default JSON changed nonfinite numeric row IDs to null after a fulfilled receipt."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed by checking the restored key before writing."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"NaN and both infinity cases."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-Q3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Confirmed oracle fixture admitted manual work outside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":"; a legal direct nested insertion reached the same peer reentry and passed."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed the driver; no product change for this item."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Protect legal callback reentry."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"R2-Q4"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Same ORC-012 gap as R2-RC2."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Duplicate of R2-RC2."}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Retain independent evidence audit."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All twelve raw items are accounted for: nine fixed and three duplicates.\nNo item is deferred or awaiting a design decision. The separate negative\ncontrols remain accepted: fire-and-forget nested automatic work after an outer\nfailure is independently admitted; awaiting a later same-Collection write in\nan earlier handler is an order dependency cycle; simultaneous cross-tab writes\nand distinct custom Storage wrapper objects have no atomic coordination law."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"law-coverage-and-calibration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Law coverage and calibration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Durable/public agreement (R2-RC1, PE1, PA3, PA1)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The LocalStorage guide\npromises active same-tab peers agree with a successful stored snapshot at the\nfulfilled persistence receipt, apart from the stated default-JSON native-value\nwriter exception and failed reads. The independent authored-row fold predicts\nthe normalized prior row and new row. The peer owner varies whether the parser\nchanges the prior token. The unchanged-token case failed at the writer/peer\nreceipt on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d5205b7"},{"type":"text","value":"; the changed-token control passed. A separate one-shot\npostwrite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getItem"},{"type":"text","value":" fault failed the successful-receipt assertion while durable\nbytes already held the row; the prewrite-fault control still rejects without\nwriting. The repaired code parses and validates custom-parser bytes before\nthe durable write, then uses that snapshot for confirmation and publication.\nThe finite law covers the named parser, one write, active same-object peers,\nreceipt and fresh restore. Arbitrary parser side effects and native browser\nscheduling remain outside this fixture's claim."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Key identity and restorable receipts (R2-PA2, PE2, Q2)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" One stored entry\nmust represent one Collection key, and a fulfilled write must permit a fresh\nrestore of its row. The malformed classifier now rejects two encodings that\ndecode to one number before readiness. The typed-key driver accepts a\nserializable string that derives NaN, plus finite number and string controls;\nfresh restore failed on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d5205b7"},{"type":"text","value":" and now passes. The default-JSON writer\ndriver tries NaN and both infinities as row IDs; on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d5205b7"},{"type":"text","value":" each fulfilled\nand wrote un-restorable data, while the oracle expected rejection and unchanged\nstorage. The repaired writer checks each changed row's JSON key round trip\nbefore "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setItem"},{"type":"text","value":". A single legacy unprefixed string key remains accepted;\nduplicate identity is invalid regardless of spelling. The fixed finite inputs\ndo not prove every user-defined key extractor or serialization shape."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accepted-work settlement (R2-Q1)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The mutations guide permits local work\naccepted before a later API error to persist. The transaction receipt cannot\nsettle until that registered work finishes. The order owner's independent fold\ncontains both accepted rows. Its driver holds an earlier automatic handler,\ncalls un-awaited manual acceptance inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mutationFn"},{"type":"text","value":", then throws. At the\nheld cut, both commit and persistence receipt were incorrectly rejected on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d5205b7"},{"type":"text","value":"; now both remain pending. After release, both report the original\nAPI error, while durable, public, and fresh-restore rows show the accepted\nwrite. The nearby successful and storage-fault manual histories protect the\nother settlement paths. This does not make multiple Storage keys atomic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legal reentry (R2-Q3)."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The peer owner now inserts directly from the\nsubscriber callback. This is an admitted production path, unlike calling\nmanual acceptance before commit. Its nested receipt and later update compare\nwriter, peer, and durable rows. The legal replacement passed before the\nproduction repair, so this item was a driver correction rather than a product\nRED case."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"oracle-guide-audit","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Oracle guide audit"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The guide and oracle opening prose state receipt, key, and\nrestoration laws and the cross-tab, parser, and native-value limits."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Expected rows come from authored operations or a malformed\nsnapshot classifier, never the adapter mirror, version cache, or decoder."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Opening prose, model helpers, bounded histories, production\ncalls, and receipt/restore assertions remain adjacent in both owners."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The finite token-change, typed-key, fault-position, and held\npredecessor axes reconstruct the reported cases and controls. Removing the\nunchanged-token, NaN, postwrite, or failure-after-acceptance member loses\nits corresponding wrong design. Invalid malformed bytes are rejected. No\nunbounded generated-history claim is made."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Real Collection insert, manual transaction, preload, same-tab\npublication, and fresh restore paths reach the named public observations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The merged pre-fix implementation failed the intended new\nassertions; the legal nested-reentry replacement passed as a negative\ncontrol. Failures were assertions, not setup errors or timeouts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Not applicable: the changed tests are fixed finite histories,\nnot an important random generated property."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Not applicable: no state was added to or removed from the\nindependent authored-row fold."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The fold's accepted row maps to a completed Storage write;\npending handler and receipt are production timing concepts observed by\ncontrolled gates, not hidden model state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withHistoryCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":" releases handlers and Collections after\nassertions while preserving the primary failure."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Durable-byte inspection and fresh Collection restore are\nseparate formulations of the receipt result. They differ from active peer\npublication; no additional shared-fault hypothesis remains for this scope."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" This record reports each applicable requirement and explicit\nnon-applicability, with the law boundaries and original/adjacent witnesses."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-013:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Changed and unchanged tokens, prewrite and postwrite faults,\nserializable versus lossy nonfinite keys, and held versus released work\ndistinguish the reusable conditional laws from plausible wrong boundaries."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-014:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" The controlled "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StorageApi"},{"type":"text","value":" preserves native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getItem"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setItem"},{"type":"text","value":" return semantics. These findings are scoped to that API seam;\ncross-tab native scheduling is not inferred from the in-memory event host."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verification","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The initial oracle-only run on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d5205b7"},{"type":"text","value":" had eight intended failures among\n92 focused tests: same-token normalization, duplicate decoded key, NaN\nrestore, three lossy nonfinite IDs, postwrite fault, and early failure\nsettlement. The neighboring changed-token and legal nested-reentry controls\npassed. After repair, the two primary oracle files passed all 92 initial\ntests. A final legacy-key control increased the peer owner to 49 passing\ntests; the broader LocalStorage run had passed 150 tests before that added\ncontrol. The full package runtime suite passed 11,390 tests before that added\ncontrol. Standalone source and test TypeScript, package build, changed-file\nlint, formatting, generated error docs, and ESM/CommonJS production error\nchecks passed. Vitest's integrated typecheck still exits nonzero on the\nunchanged "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src/collection/index.ts:961"},{"type":"text","value":" overload diagnostic, while both\nstandalone TypeScript checks pass; it is not reported as a green integrated\ngate. CI results on the pushed head are tracked separately."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"localstorage-second-oracle-review-2026-10-08","text":"LocalStorage second oracle review, 2026-10-08","level":1},{"id":"reviewer-assessment-and-loss-audit","text":"Reviewer assessment and loss audit","level":2},{"id":"law-coverage-and-calibration","text":"Law coverage and calibration","level":2},{"id":"oracle-guide-audit","text":"Oracle guide audit","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Verification","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# LocalStorage second oracle review, 2026-10-08\n\nThe four independent reviews examined PR #2074 at\n`9344612bed911a364af6c9c3f9b023487cf6979a`. The normal merge of\n`origin/main` produced `d5205b792e34f0f6f0282b7811a5c7de712ae0cd`;\nall changed oracle histories were first run against that merged implementation.\nThe repaired implementation reviewed here is\n`1f103c8504d135c1fa35215efba9d7ebc5db92e2` (the versioned record itself is a subsequent documentation-only\ncommit).\nThe append-only raw intake is `/private/tmp/pr2074-round2-review-ledger.md`.\n\n## Reviewer assessment and loss audit\n\nThese reviews were accurate and useful. Three reviewers independently found\nthe same parser-normalization gap, which raises confidence but counts as one\nproduct cause. The queue reviewer separated accepted local work from a later\nAPI failure rather than incorrectly demanding cancellation. The key findings\nalso distinguished a serializable NaN-derived key from a nonfinite value that\nJSON cannot round-trip. Proposed fixes were mostly narrow; canonical-key\nrejection was stronger than the existing legacy compatibility contract.\nTechnical depth and signal-to-noise were high. **Hire recommendation: yes.**\n\n| ID | Technical verdict and evidence | PR action | Durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R2-RC1 | Confirmed: unchanged-token normalization of an untouched row left writer and peer stale at the fulfilled receipt. | Fixed in peer oracle and content comparison. | Paired changed/unchanged token histories. |\n| R2-PE1 | Same confirmed cause, independently probed. | Duplicate of R2-RC1. | Retain independent receipt evidence. |\n| R2-PA1 | Confirmed: postwrite custom-parser read fault rejected a durable write. | Fixed by validating serialized custom-parser bytes before `setItem`. | Prewrite and postwrite fault-position histories. |\n| R2-Q1 | Confirmed: `mutationFn` failure settled before its previously accepted queued write. The later write itself is allowed by the mutations guide. | Fixed in transaction settlement and order oracle. | Held and released receipt cuts with durable, public and restore observations. |\n| R2-PA2 | Confirmed: `n:1` and `n:01` silently collapsed into one Map identity. | Fixed by rejecting duplicate decoded identities before readiness. | Malformed snapshot grammar retains legacy single-key support. |\n| R2-PE2 | Confirmed: a serializable row deriving numeric NaN as its Collection key wrote successfully but could not restore. | Fixed with SameValueZero identity validation. | NaN, finite number, and string controls. |\n| R2-PE3 | Confirmed wording gap: guide implied legacy unprefixed keys were invalid. | Fixed guide wording. | Explicit compatibility boundary. |\n| R2-RC2 | Confirmed review evidence gap for the previous changed head. | Fixed with this versioned exact-implementation review. | ORC-012 outcomes below. |\n| R2-PA3 | Same confirmed normalization cause, independently probed. | Duplicate of R2-RC1. | Retain the parser-focused probe. |\n| R2-Q2 | Confirmed: default JSON changed nonfinite numeric row IDs to null after a fulfilled receipt. | Fixed by checking the restored key before writing. | NaN and both infinity cases. |\n| R2-Q3 | Confirmed oracle fixture admitted manual work outside `mutationFn`; a legal direct nested insertion reached the same peer reentry and passed. | Fixed the driver; no product change for this item. | Protect legal callback reentry. |\n| R2-Q4 | Same ORC-012 gap as R2-RC2. | Duplicate of R2-RC2. | Retain independent evidence audit. |\n\nAll twelve raw items are accounted for: nine fixed and three duplicates.\nNo item is deferred or awaiting a design decision. The separate negative\ncontrols remain accepted: fire-and-forget nested automatic work after an outer\nfailure is independently admitted; awaiting a later same-Collection write in\nan earlier handler is an order dependency cycle; simultaneous cross-tab writes\nand distinct custom Storage wrapper objects have no atomic coordination law.\n\n## Law coverage and calibration\n\n**Durable/public agreement (R2-RC1, PE1, PA3, PA1).** The LocalStorage guide\npromises active same-tab peers agree with a successful stored snapshot at the\nfulfilled persistence receipt, apart from the stated default-JSON native-value\nwriter exception and failed reads. The independent authored-row fold predicts\nthe normalized prior row and new row. The peer owner varies whether the parser\nchanges the prior token. The unchanged-token case failed at the writer/peer\nreceipt on `d5205b7`; the changed-token control passed. A separate one-shot\npostwrite `getItem` fault failed the successful-receipt assertion while durable\nbytes already held the row; the prewrite-fault control still rejects without\nwriting. The repaired code parses and validates custom-parser bytes before\nthe durable write, then uses that snapshot for confirmation and publication.\nThe finite law covers the named parser, one write, active same-object peers,\nreceipt and fresh restore. Arbitrary parser side effects and native browser\nscheduling remain outside this fixture's claim.\n\n**Key identity and restorable receipts (R2-PA2, PE2, Q2).** One stored entry\nmust represent one Collection key, and a fulfilled write must permit a fresh\nrestore of its row. The malformed classifier now rejects two encodings that\ndecode to one number before readiness. The typed-key driver accepts a\nserializable string that derives NaN, plus finite number and string controls;\nfresh restore failed on `d5205b7` and now passes. The default-JSON writer\ndriver tries NaN and both infinities as row IDs; on `d5205b7` each fulfilled\nand wrote un-restorable data, while the oracle expected rejection and unchanged\nstorage. The repaired writer checks each changed row's JSON key round trip\nbefore `setItem`. A single legacy unprefixed string key remains accepted;\nduplicate identity is invalid regardless of spelling. The fixed finite inputs\ndo not prove every user-defined key extractor or serialization shape.\n\n**Accepted-work settlement (R2-Q1).** The mutations guide permits local work\naccepted before a later API error to persist. The transaction receipt cannot\nsettle until that registered work finishes. The order owner's independent fold\ncontains both accepted rows. Its driver holds an earlier automatic handler,\ncalls un-awaited manual acceptance inside `mutationFn`, then throws. At the\nheld cut, both commit and persistence receipt were incorrectly rejected on\n`d5205b7`; now both remain pending. After release, both report the original\nAPI error, while durable, public, and fresh-restore rows show the accepted\nwrite. The nearby successful and storage-fault manual histories protect the\nother settlement paths. This does not make multiple Storage keys atomic.\n\n**Legal reentry (R2-Q3).** The peer owner now inserts directly from the\nsubscriber callback. This is an admitted production path, unlike calling\nmanual acceptance before commit. Its nested receipt and later update compare\nwriter, peer, and durable rows. The legal replacement passed before the\nproduction repair, so this item was a driver correction rather than a product\nRED case.\n\n## Oracle guide audit\n\n- **ORC-001:** The guide and oracle opening prose state receipt, key, and\n  restoration laws and the cross-tab, parser, and native-value limits.\n- **ORC-002:** Expected rows come from authored operations or a malformed\n  snapshot classifier, never the adapter mirror, version cache, or decoder.\n- **ORC-003:** Opening prose, model helpers, bounded histories, production\n  calls, and receipt/restore assertions remain adjacent in both owners.\n- **ORC-004:** The finite token-change, typed-key, fault-position, and held\n  predecessor axes reconstruct the reported cases and controls. Removing the\n  unchanged-token, NaN, postwrite, or failure-after-acceptance member loses\n  its corresponding wrong design. Invalid malformed bytes are rejected. No\n  unbounded generated-history claim is made.\n- **ORC-005:** Real Collection insert, manual transaction, preload, same-tab\n  publication, and fresh restore paths reach the named public observations.\n- **ORC-006:** The merged pre-fix implementation failed the intended new\n  assertions; the legal nested-reentry replacement passed as a negative\n  control. Failures were assertions, not setup errors or timeouts.\n- **ORC-007:** Not applicable: the changed tests are fixed finite histories,\n  not an important random generated property.\n- **ORC-008:** Not applicable: no state was added to or removed from the\n  independent authored-row fold.\n- **ORC-009:** The fold's accepted row maps to a completed Storage write;\n  pending handler and receipt are production timing concepts observed by\n  controlled gates, not hidden model state.\n- **ORC-010:** `withHistoryCleanup` releases handlers and Collections after\n  assertions while preserving the primary failure.\n- **ORC-011:** Durable-byte inspection and fresh Collection restore are\n  separate formulations of the receipt result. They differ from active peer\n  publication; no additional shared-fault hypothesis remains for this scope.\n- **ORC-012:** This record reports each applicable requirement and explicit\n  non-applicability, with the law boundaries and original/adjacent witnesses.\n- **ORC-013:** Changed and unchanged tokens, prewrite and postwrite faults,\n  serializable versus lossy nonfinite keys, and held versus released work\n  distinguish the reusable conditional laws from plausible wrong boundaries.\n- **ORC-014:** The controlled `StorageApi` preserves native `getItem` and\n  `setItem` return semantics. These findings are scoped to that API seam;\n  cross-tab native scheduling is not inferred from the in-memory event host.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe initial oracle-only run on `d5205b7` had eight intended failures among\n92 focused tests: same-token normalization, duplicate decoded key, NaN\nrestore, three lossy nonfinite IDs, postwrite fault, and early failure\nsettlement. The neighboring changed-token and legal nested-reentry controls\npassed. After repair, the two primary oracle files passed all 92 initial\ntests. A final legacy-key control increased the peer owner to 49 passing\ntests; the broader LocalStorage run had passed 150 tests before that added\ncontrol. The full package runtime suite passed 11,390 tests before that added\ncontrol. Standalone source and test TypeScript, package build, changed-file\nlint, formatting, generated error docs, and ESM/CommonJS production error\nchecks passed. Vitest's integrated typecheck still exits nonzero on the\nunchanged `src/collection/index.ts:961` overload diagnostic, while both\nstandalone TypeScript checks pass; it is not reported as a green integrated\ngate. CI results on the pushed head are tracked separately.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# LocalStorage second oracle review, 2026-10-08\n\nThe four independent reviews examined PR #2074 at\n`9344612bed911a364af6c9c3f9b023487cf6979a`. The normal merge of\n`origin/main` produced `d5205b792e34f0f6f0282b7811a5c7de712ae0cd`;\nall changed oracle histories were first run against that merged implementation.\nThe repaired implementation reviewed here is\n`1f103c8504d135c1fa35215efba9d7ebc5db92e2` (the versioned record itself is a subsequent documentation-only\ncommit).\nThe append-only raw intake is `/private/tmp/pr2074-round2-review-ledger.md`.\n\n## Reviewer assessment and loss audit\n\nThese reviews were accurate and useful. Three reviewers independently found\nthe same parser-normalization gap, which raises confidence but counts as one\nproduct cause. The queue reviewer separated accepted local work from a later\nAPI failure rather than incorrectly demanding cancellation. The key findings\nalso distinguished a serializable NaN-derived key from a nonfinite value that\nJSON cannot round-trip. Proposed fixes were mostly narrow; canonical-key\nrejection was stronger than the existing legacy compatibility contract.\nTechnical depth and signal-to-noise were high. **Hire recommendation: yes.**\n\n| ID | Technical verdict and evidence | PR action | Durable value |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R2-RC1 | Confirmed: unchanged-token normalization of an untouched row left writer and peer stale at the fulfilled receipt. | Fixed in peer oracle and content comparison. | Paired changed/unchanged token histories. |\n| R2-PE1 | Same confirmed cause, independently probed. | Duplicate of R2-RC1. | Retain independent receipt evidence. |\n| R2-PA1 | Confirmed: postwrite custom-parser read fault rejected a durable write. | Fixed by validating serialized custom-parser bytes before `setItem`. | Prewrite and postwrite fault-position histories. |\n| R2-Q1 | Confirmed: `mutationFn` failure settled before its previously accepted queued write. The later write itself is allowed by the mutations guide. | Fixed in transaction settlement and order oracle. | Held and released receipt cuts with durable, public and restore observations. |\n| R2-PA2 | Confirmed: `n:1` and `n:01` silently collapsed into one Map identity. | Fixed by rejecting duplicate decoded identities before readiness. | Malformed snapshot grammar retains legacy single-key support. |\n| R2-PE2 | Confirmed: a serializable row deriving numeric NaN as its Collection key wrote successfully but could not restore. | Fixed with SameValueZero identity validation. | NaN, finite number, and string controls. |\n| R2-PE3 | Confirmed wording gap: guide implied legacy unprefixed keys were invalid. | Fixed guide wording. | Explicit compatibility boundary. |\n| R2-RC2 | Confirmed review evidence gap for the previous changed head. | Fixed with this versioned exact-implementation review. | ORC-012 outcomes below. |\n| R2-PA3 | Same confirmed normalization cause, independently probed. | Duplicate of R2-RC1. | Retain the parser-focused probe. |\n| R2-Q2 | Confirmed: default JSON changed nonfinite numeric row IDs to null after a fulfilled receipt. | Fixed by checking the restored key before writing. | NaN and both infinity cases. |\n| R2-Q3 | Confirmed oracle fixture admitted manual work outside `mutationFn`; a legal direct nested insertion reached the same peer reentry and passed. | Fixed the driver; no product change for this item. | Protect legal callback reentry. |\n| R2-Q4 | Same ORC-012 gap as R2-RC2. | Duplicate of R2-RC2. | Retain independent evidence audit. |\n\nAll twelve raw items are accounted for: nine fixed and three duplicates.\nNo item is deferred or awaiting a design decision. The separate negative\ncontrols remain accepted: fire-and-forget nested automatic work after an outer\nfailure is independently admitted; awaiting a later same-Collection write in\nan earlier handler is an order dependency cycle; simultaneous cross-tab writes\nand distinct custom Storage wrapper objects have no atomic coordination law.\n\n## Law coverage and calibration\n\n**Durable/public agreement (R2-RC1, PE1, PA3, PA1).** The LocalStorage guide\npromises active same-tab peers agree with a successful stored snapshot at the\nfulfilled persistence receipt, apart from the stated default-JSON native-value\nwriter exception and failed reads. The independent authored-row fold predicts\nthe normalized prior row and new row. The peer owner varies whether the parser\nchanges the prior token. The unchanged-token case failed at the writer/peer\nreceipt on `d5205b7`; the changed-token control passed. A separate one-shot\npostwrite `getItem` fault failed the successful-receipt assertion while durable\nbytes already held the row; the prewrite-fault control still rejects without\nwriting. The repaired code parses and validates custom-parser bytes before\nthe durable write, then uses that snapshot for confirmation and publication.\nThe finite law covers the named parser, one write, active same-object peers,\nreceipt and fresh restore. Arbitrary parser side effects and native browser\nscheduling remain outside this fixture's claim.\n\n**Key identity and restorable receipts (R2-PA2, PE2, Q2).** One stored entry\nmust represent one Collection key, and a fulfilled write must permit a fresh\nrestore of its row. The malformed classifier now rejects two encodings that\ndecode to one number before readiness. The typed-key driver accepts a\nserializable string that derives NaN, plus finite number and string controls;\nfresh restore failed on `d5205b7` and now passes. The default-JSON writer\ndriver tries NaN and both infinities as row IDs; on `d5205b7` each fulfilled\nand wrote un-restorable data, while the oracle expected rejection and unchanged\nstorage. The repaired writer checks each changed row's JSON key round trip\nbefore `setItem`. A single legacy unprefixed string key remains accepted;\nduplicate identity is invalid regardless of spelling. The fixed finite inputs\ndo not prove every user-defined key extractor or serialization shape.\n\n**Accepted-work settlement (R2-Q1).** The mutations guide permits local work\naccepted before a later API error to persist. The transaction receipt cannot\nsettle until that registered work finishes. The order owner's independent fold\ncontains both accepted rows. Its driver holds an earlier automatic handler,\ncalls un-awaited manual acceptance inside `mutationFn`, then throws. At the\nheld cut, both commit and persistence receipt were incorrectly rejected on\n`d5205b7`; now both remain pending. After release, both report the original\nAPI error, while durable, public, and fresh-restore rows show the accepted\nwrite. The nearby successful and storage-fault manual histories protect the\nother settlement paths. This does not make multiple Storage keys atomic.\n\n**Legal reentry (R2-Q3).** The peer owner now inserts directly from the\nsubscriber callback. This is an admitted production path, unlike calling\nmanual acceptance before commit. Its nested receipt and later update compare\nwriter, peer, and durable rows. The legal replacement passed before the\nproduction repair, so this item was a driver correction rather than a product\nRED case.\n\n## Oracle guide audit\n\n- **ORC-001:** The guide and oracle opening prose state receipt, key, and\n  restoration laws and the cross-tab, parser, and native-value limits.\n- **ORC-002:** Expected rows come from authored operations or a malformed\n  snapshot classifier, never the adapter mirror, version cache, or decoder.\n- **ORC-003:** Opening prose, model helpers, bounded histories, production\n  calls, and receipt/restore assertions remain adjacent in both owners.\n- **ORC-004:** The finite token-change, typed-key, fault-position, and held\n  predecessor axes reconstruct the reported cases and controls. Removing the\n  unchanged-token, NaN, postwrite, or failure-after-acceptance member loses\n  its corresponding wrong design. Invalid malformed bytes are rejected. No\n  unbounded generated-history claim is made.\n- **ORC-005:** Real Collection insert, manual transaction, preload, same-tab\n  publication, and fresh restore paths reach the named public observations.\n- **ORC-006:** The merged pre-fix implementation failed the intended new\n  assertions; the legal nested-reentry replacement passed as a negative\n  control. Failures were assertions, not setup errors or timeouts.\n- **ORC-007:** Not applicable: the changed tests are fixed finite histories,\n  not an important random generated property.\n- **ORC-008:** Not applicable: no state was added to or removed from the\n  independent authored-row fold.\n- **ORC-009:** The fold's accepted row maps to a completed Storage write;\n  pending handler and receipt are production timing concepts observed by\n  controlled gates, not hidden model state.\n- **ORC-010:** `withHistoryCleanup` releases handlers and Collections after\n  assertions while preserving the primary failure.\n- **ORC-011:** Durable-byte inspection and fresh Collection restore are\n  separate formulations of the receipt result. They differ from active peer\n  publication; no additional shared-fault hypothesis remains for this scope.\n- **ORC-012:** This record reports each applicable requirement and explicit\n  non-applicability, with the law boundaries and original/adjacent witnesses.\n- **ORC-013:** Changed and unchanged tokens, prewrite and postwrite faults,\n  serializable versus lossy nonfinite keys, and held versus released work\n  distinguish the reusable conditional laws from plausible wrong boundaries.\n- **ORC-014:** The controlled `StorageApi` preserves native `getItem` and\n  `setItem` return semantics. These findings are scoped to that API seam;\n  cross-tab native scheduling is not inferred from the in-memory event host.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe initial oracle-only run on `d5205b7` had eight intended failures among\n92 focused tests: same-token normalization, duplicate decoded key, NaN\nrestore, three lossy nonfinite IDs, postwrite fault, and early failure\nsettlement. The neighboring changed-token and legal nested-reentry controls\npassed. After repair, the two primary oracle files passed all 92 initial\ntests. A final legacy-key control increased the peer owner to 49 passing\ntests; the broader LocalStorage run had passed 150 tests before that added\ncontrol. The full package runtime suite passed 11,390 tests before that added\ncontrol. Standalone source and test TypeScript, package build, changed-file\nlint, formatting, generated error docs, and ESM/CommonJS production error\nchecks passed. Vitest's integrated typecheck still exits nonzero on the\nunchanged `src/collection/index.ts:961` overload diagnostic, while both\nstandalone TypeScript checks pass; it is not reported as a green integrated\ngate. CI results on the pushed head are tracked separately.\n"}
